---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/Faq.astro";
import { faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema, type FaqItem } from "../../lib/schema";
import { PRICE, PRODUCT } from "../../consts";

const crumbs = [
	{ label: "Home", href: "/" },
	{ label: "Compare", href: "/compare/" },
	{ label: "Vizard alternative", href: "/compare/vizard-alternative/" },
];

const CHECKED = "July 2026";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
	{
		q: "Is Soft Clipper a good Vizard alternative?",
		a: `If the thing that bothers you about Vizard is the monthly bill and the credit counter, yes — Soft Clipper is ${PRICE.display} once, renders on your own PC, and has no quota of any kind. If what you value is uploading from a phone, collaborating with a team, or posting straight to your social accounts, Vizard does those and Soft Clipper does not.`,
	},
	{
		q: "What does Vizard cost?",
		a: `As of ${CHECKED}, Vizard publishes a free tier with a small monthly credit allowance, 720p exports and short-lived storage, and paid Creator and Business tiers. Paying monthly costs roughly twice what paying for a year up front does — around $29 a month against about $14.50 a month on the annual plan for Creator, with Business higher again. Check their pricing page before you decide; cloud tools change tiers often.`,
	},
	{
		q: "What are credits and why do they matter?",
		a: "Cloud clip tools meter you, because every minute you upload costs them compute. Credits or upload minutes are that meter. It means the cost of a busy month is not the number on the pricing page, and a back catalogue you want to work through all at once is exactly the case the meter punishes. Soft Clipper has no meter: the limit is how fast your own PC encodes video.",
	},
	{
		q: "Does my video get uploaded?",
		a: "To Vizard, yes — that is how a cloud tool works, and there is nothing sinister about it. With Soft Clipper the video file never leaves your machine. Audio goes out for transcription if you use a cloud AI key, and even that stops if you switch on local transcription.",
	},
	{
		q: "Which one should a team pick?",
		a: "Vizard, in most cases. It has shared workspaces, brand kits and per-seat billing built for exactly that. Soft Clipper is one licence per machine — fine for an agency that buys one per editor, but there is no shared library and no review flow.",
	},
];

const rows = [
	{ f: "Price", a: `${PRICE.display} one-time`, b: "Monthly or annual subscription" },
	{ f: "Upload or render limits", a: "None — your PC is the limit", b: "Credit / minute allowance per month" },
	{ f: "Where rendering happens", a: "Your PC", b: "Their servers" },
	{ f: "Your video leaves your machine", a: "No — audio only, and optional", b: "Yes, the whole file" },
	{ f: "Works offline", a: "Cutting, reframing and export, yes", b: "No" },
	{ f: "Watermark", a: "Never — there is no free tier", b: "On the free tier" },
	{ f: "Export quality on the free tier", a: "n/a", b: "Reduced" },
	{ f: "Speaker-tracking reframe", a: "Yes", b: "Yes" },
	{ f: "Burned-in captions", a: "Yes, six styles", b: "Yes" },
	{ f: "Aspect ratios", a: PRODUCT.ratios, b: "Vertical, square, landscape" },
	{ f: "Team workspaces and brand kits", a: "No", b: "Yes" },
	{ f: "Publish straight to social accounts", a: "No", b: "Yes" },
	{ f: "Works from a phone or tablet", a: "No", b: "Yes, in a browser" },
	{ f: "Platform", a: PRODUCT.platforms, b: "Any browser" },
	{ f: "Keeps working if the company stops trading", a: "Yes — it is on your disk", b: "No" },
];
---

<Page
	title="Soft Clipper vs Vizard"
	heading="Soft Clipper as a Vizard alternative"
	description={`An honest comparison of a one-time desktop clip tool and a metered cloud one. Credits, uploads, offline use and what each costs over two years. Pricing checked ${CHECKED}.`}
	intro="Vizard is a good cloud tool. The question is not whether it works — it does — but whether renting video processing by the minute is the right shape for what you are doing."
	eyebrow="Comparison"
	crumbs={crumbs}
	schema={[faqSchema(faqs), breadcrumbSchema(crumbs)]}
>
	<section class="pt-0">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="answer">
				<strong>Short answer:</strong> if you work in a team, post from a phone, or want
				publishing built in, use Vizard. If you are one person with a back catalogue and a
				computer that already renders video, Soft Clipper costs {PRICE.display} once and
				never counts your minutes.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Side by side</h2>
			<p class="lead">
				Checked {CHECKED} against Vizard's published material. Cloud tiers change often —
				treat the pricing line as a shape, not a quote, and read theirs before you buy.
			</p>
			<div class="table-scroll mt-lg">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Soft Clipper</th><th>Vizard</th></tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{
							rows.map((r) => (
								<tr>
									<td><strong>{r.f}</strong></td>
									<td>{r.a}</td>
									<td>{r.b}</td>
								</tr>
							))
						}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="grid grid-2">
				<div class="card">
					<h2>Where Vizard wins</h2>
					<ul class="checks">
						<li><strong>It runs anywhere there is a browser</strong> — including a Chromebook and a phone. Soft Clipper needs {PRODUCT.platforms}.</li>
						<li><strong>Teams.</strong> Shared workspaces, brand kits and per-seat billing are built in.</li>
						<li><strong>It posts for you.</strong> Connect the accounts and publish from the same screen.</li>
						<li><strong>No hardware requirement.</strong> Rendering happens on their machines, so a slow laptop does not slow you down.</li>
						<li><strong>Nothing to install</strong> and nothing to update.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h2>Where Soft Clipper wins</h2>
					<ul class="checks">
						<li><strong>{PRICE.display} once.</strong> No renewal, no card on file, no price rise next year.</li>
						<li><strong>No credits.</strong> Clip a hundred episodes in a weekend if you like — nothing counts them.</li>
						<li><strong>Your video stays on your disk.</strong> Only audio leaves, and only if you let it.</li>
						<li><strong>No upload wait.</strong> A two-hour recording does not have to travel anywhere first.</li>
						<li><strong>It still works</strong> if the internet is down, or if we stop trading.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<h2>The number that actually decides it</h2>
			</div>
			<p class="lead">
				A subscription is not expensive because of its monthly price; it is expensive because
				it does not stop. At around ${PRICE.competitorMonthly} a month, two years of a cloud
				clip tool is roughly ${PRICE.competitorMonthly * 24} — against {PRICE.display} once.
				That gap is the whole argument, and it only matters if you are still clipping in two
				years. If this is a one-off project, a month of Vizard is cheaper than either.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-lg">
				<a href="/compare/one-time-vs-subscription/">See the full two-year cost comparison →</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<h2>The upload is not just a privacy question</h2>
			</div>
			<p class="lead">
				Sending a two-hour recording to a server is a real cost even when you trust the
				server completely: it is your upstream bandwidth, and it is time you sit and wait.
				On a domestic connection a 4 GB file is a coffee break before any work starts. Soft
				Clipper reads the file where it already is. That is not a privacy feature, it is a
				speed one — the privacy is a side effect.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
			<Faq items={faqs} />
		</div>
	</section>
</Page>

<style>
	.pt-0 {
		padding-top: 8px;
	}
	.card h2 {
		font-size: 1.2rem;
		margin-bottom: 14px;
	}
</style>
